10:46:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your professor is likely right—but “array operations are O(n)” is an oversimplification unless they specified **which operation**. Different operations on an array have different complexities: | Operation | Array complexity | Why | |---|---:|---| | Read `arr[i]` | O(1) | Compute address from index | | Write `arr[i] = x` | O(1) | Same reason | | Search for value `x` in unsorted array | O(n) | May inspect every element | | Traverse / print all items | O(n) | Must visit every item | | Insert at beginning/middle | O(n) | Shift later elements right | | Delete at beginning/middle | O(n) | Shift later elements left | | Append to a fixed-capacity array with room | O(1) | Write at end | | Append to a dynamic array | amortized O(1) | Usually O(1), occasionally resize/copy O(n) | So if the whiteboard showed something like this: ```text [ A ][ B ][ C ][ D ][ E ] ``` and then inserted something at the front: ```text insert X at index 0 ``` the array has to become: ```text [ X ][ A ][ B ][ C ][ D ][ E ] ``` That requires moving `A`, `B`, `C`, `D`, and `E`, so it is O(n). Or if it showed a search: ```text find "D" ``` then you may have to inspect: ```text A → B → C → D ``` and in the worst case inspect all `n` elements. Also O(n). But if it showed: ```text get the element at index 3 ``` then the computer directly computes its address. That specific operation is O(1). The important correction is: > An **array data structure** is not simply “O(1)” or “O(n).” > Its complexity depends on the operation being performed. Hash tables are the same way: | Operation | Hash table complexity | |---|---:| | Lookup by key | Expected O(1) | | Insert by key | Expected O(1) | | Delete by key | Expected O(1) | | Iterate over every item | O(n) | | Bad collisions / adversarial worst case | O(n) in many implementations | So when someone says “hash table lookup is O(1),” they mean: ```text lookup(table, key) ``` not every conceivable hash-table operation. And when someone says “array lookup is O(n),” they often really mean: ```text search(array, value) ``` rather than: ```text array[index] ``` Those are two different kinds of lookup: ```text array[123] // “I know where it is.” O(1) find(array, "Alice") // “I know what it is, not where.” O(n) table["Alice"] // Hash key gives likely location. Expected O(1) ``` Your professor’s diagram probably emphasized the operations where arrays require scanning or shifting, which are indeed O(n).
