10:46:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Sometimes, yes—natural-language terminology is sloppy here. In algorithm discussions, though, **“array access” usually has a specific technical meaning**: ```text arr[i] ``` meaning “read or write the element at a known index.” That is O(1). If someone means “go through the array and get data out,” the more precise terms are: - **traversal**: visit every element → O(n) - **search**: find an element matching a value/predicate → O(n) for an unsorted array - **iteration**: loop through elements → O(n) For example: ```python x = arr[500] # access by index: O(1) for x in arr: # traversal: O(n) print(x) if "Alice" in arr: # search: O(n), generally ... ``` So both statements can be reasonable depending on what “accessing” meant: - “Getting an element at a known array index is O(1).” - “Going through an array to retrieve/process its contents is O(n).” The confusion arises because “look up” can mean either: 1. **I know its address/index; give me the item there.** 2. **I know the item/key/value; find where it is.** Arrays are excellent at #1. Hash tables are designed for #2, provided you use a key. A hash table itself also uses an underlying array internally. The hash function turns a key into an array index: ```text "alice" ↓ hash some number ↓ map into table size bucket index ↓ underlyingArray[bucket index] ``` That final array access is O(1). The benefit is that hashing lets you derive an index from `"alice"` without scanning all entries first. So your intuition is valid in ordinary language. In CS notation, people should ideally say **“indexed access”** versus **“linear search/traversal”** to avoid exactly this ambiguity.
