10:47:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a fair practical observation. If your task is: ```text “Do something to every item in this array” ``` then yes, it is necessarily O(n), because there are `n` items to process. No data structure—including a hash table—can let you inspect or transform all `n` items in O(1). ```python for user in users: send_email(user) ``` is O(n), whether `users` is an array, hash table, linked list, database result, etc. The O(1) claim matters for a different workload: ```text “Given one key, find just that one item.” ``` For example: ```python # Need the user with ID 839274: user = users_by_id[839274] ``` With a hash table/dictionary, that is expected O(1). With an array of user objects: ```python for user in users: if user.id == 839274: return user ``` that is O(n). ## In real projects, you often do both You might store users in a list because you want to display or process all of them: ```python for user in users: render_user(user) ``` But also maintain a hash table for frequent single-user lookups: ```python users_by_id[user.id] = user ``` Then: ```python # Process all users: for user in users: # O(n) ... # Find one user: user = users_by_id[target_id] # expected O(1) ``` Sometimes the same objects are stored in both structures: ```text users list: [ User(id=1), User(id=2), User(id=3) ] users_by_id map: 1 → same User object 2 → same User object 3 → same User object ``` That costs extra memory and bookkeeping, but can make the common operations fast. ## Why it still matters Suppose you have 1,000,000 users and process 1,000 user-ID requests. - Scanning an array for each request: roughly up to 1,000 × 1,000,000 checks → potentially O(1 billion). - Hash-table lookup for each request: roughly 1,000 lookups → expected O(1,000). If you only loop over the complete dataset once, a hash table does not change the fact that that loop is O(n). But if you repeatedly ask “find this specific item,” the difference becomes huge. So the practical version is: > If your job inherently touches every item, your job is O(n). > Hash tables help when you need to find or update a small number of particular items without touching every item.
