10:47:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There is definitely an in-between: **your understanding is correct for the work you’ve been doing**, while “array access is O(1)” is correct for a narrower, technical operation. The missing phrase is: > **Complexity belongs to an operation/algorithm, not to an array by itself.** An array is not “an O(n) thing” or “an O(1) thing.” For example, given: ```python users = [user0, user1, user2, ...] ``` These are all different operations: ```python users[700_000] # O(1): retrieve one known position len(users) # O(1): usually stored as metadata for user in users: # O(n): visit every user process(user) find_user_by_id(...) # O(n): if users is just an unsorted array ``` Your real projects likely mostly involve the last two kinds: process all records, render all rows, validate all inputs, filter data, calculate totals, etc. Those are O(n), because the requirement itself says “consider all the data.” That does **not** mean the language secretly walks through 700,000 elements when executing: ```python users[700_000] ``` It goes directly to that element. But direct indexing is often not the dominant thing your application needs to do. Likewise, a hash table does not make a whole job O(1): ```python for key, value in table.items(): process(value) ``` That is O(n), exactly like looping over an array. It helps specifically for this kind of task: ```python user = users_by_id[target_id] ``` instead of: ```python for user in users: if user.id == target_id: return user ``` So neither “every array is O(n)” nor “every course/job got it wrong” is right. The precise version is: - **Reading an array element at a known index:** O(1) - **Searching or processing an entire array:** O(n) - **Looking up one item in a hash table by key:** expected O(1) - **Processing every item in a hash table:** O(n) Your day-to-day experience is primarily with O(n) workflows because most useful application work really does need to touch many items. Big-O claims about hash tables and arrays are still useful because they distinguish the cases where you need **one particular item** from the cases where you need **all items**.
