10:50:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. An operation’s complexity is based on what that operation itself has to do, not on the fact that it belongs to a structure that is often used in O(n) workflows. For example: ```python total = 0 for i in range(len(arr)): total += arr[i] ``` The whole loop is O(n), because it performs `n` indexed reads. But each individual: ```python arr[i] ``` is still O(1) in the standard model. Doing an O(1) thing `n` times gives O(n): ```text n × O(1) = O(n) ``` Same idea: - A hash table can look up one key in expected O(1). - Looping through all keys in that hash table is O(n). - That does not make each individual hash-table lookup O(n). Or with arithmetic: ```python sum = 0 for each of n numbers: sum += number ``` Addition is constant-time in the simplified model, but adding `n` numbers is O(n). The overall task does not change the complexity of one addition. Your practical point remains valid: if your feature needs to inspect every item, then the feature is O(n), and direct indexing does not save it. But it does not follow that indexing itself is O(n). It just means indexing is not the bottleneck or the relevant operation for that feature. And at the hardware level, indexed reads can have variable latency due to caches, pages, and memory pressure. That makes “O(1)” an abstraction, not a claim of zero cost. The conventional claim is only that accessing `arr[i]` does not inherently require visiting the preceding `i - 1` array elements.
