10:50:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. You do not need to assume everything you learned is secretly wrong. Most likely, what you learned remains right; the issue is just that complexity statements need an operation attached to them. For arrays, the standard rules are stable and useful: - Loop through `n` items: O(n) - Search an unsorted array: O(n) - Insert/delete near the front or middle: O(n), because elements shift - Copy an array: O(n) - Nested full loops: often O(n²) - Read/write at a known index: O(1) in the standard model That last one is not a trapdoor that undermines the rest. It is simply why arrays are useful for things like buffers, matrices, image pixels, lookup tables, and storing data by position. A good practical habit is: > When someone gives a Big-O claim, ask “for which operation, and under what assumptions?” That is not meant to invalidate your experience. It prevents misleading summaries like “arrays are O(n)” or “hash maps are O(1)” from being treated as universal facts. For real code, your main instincts are still sound: loops over data cost proportional to the amount of data, repeated scans can become expensive, and correctness/security usually matter before micro-optimizing data structure choices.
