10:51:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a good instinct. Do **not** treat O(1) as “free.” Treat it as: > One indexed read has bounded algorithmic cost, but doing lots of reads, causing cache misses, allocating memory, calling APIs, or nesting loops can still make code slow. A safe practical mental model is: - `arr[i]` is cheap enough to use normally. - Scanning `n` elements costs O(n). - Repeating a scan inside another loop can become O(n²). - Hash-map lookup avoids scans for key-based retrieval, but has hashing, allocation, collision, and cache-locality costs. - Measure hot paths with realistic data before optimizing. For example, this is fine: ```python for i in range(len(items)): render(items[i]) ``` It is O(n), not because indexing is expensive, but because you render `n` items. This may become a problem: ```python for order in orders: customer = next(c for c in customers if c.id == order.customer_id) ``` If there are `n` orders and `m` customers, that can be O(n × m). Building a lookup map once may help: ```python customers_by_id = {c.id: c for c in customers} # O(m) for order in orders: customer = customers_by_id[order.customer_id] # expected O(1) each ``` Overall: expected O(m + n), with practical memory/cache tradeoffs. So the useful takeaway is not “array indexing is free.” It is: > Count the amount of data your whole feature touches, watch for repeated searches and nested loops, and profile when the data size or request rate makes it important. That mindset avoids the scalability mistake you are worried about while still using the standard terminology accurately.
