10:52:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It proves that **your overall workload got slower as the array got bigger**—which is very often O(n) or worse. That is a real observation. But it does not by itself prove that one indexed read is O(n). With huge arrays, things can slow down because you are likely doing one or more of these: - looping through more elements, - copying/filtering/sorting more data, - doing repeated scans, - triggering more allocations or garbage collection, - missing CPU caches more often, - using more memory pages or even swapping. For example: ```python for x in huge_array: process(x) ``` gets slower as `huge_array` grows because it performs more work: O(n). But: ```python x = huge_array[500] ``` does not need to inspect all prior elements. It may be slower than the same access in a tiny, cache-resident array due to the memory hierarchy, but it is not *algorithmically required* to do work proportional to every element in the array. So the practical takeaway is: > Bigger arrays absolutely can make programs slower. > Usually that is because the program touches, copies, searches, or otherwise manages more of the array—not because every individual index operation walks through the array. Your instinct to be cautious with huge collections is right. Just diagnose the whole operation—especially loops and repeated searches—rather than assigning the whole slowdown to `arr[i]` itself.
