10:52:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may feel simpler, but it will lead you to the wrong design choices in cases where direct indexing or key lookup matters. A simple, accurate rule is: > **Work is proportional to the number of elements you actually touch.** Then you do not have to memorize “arrays are O(n)” or “arrays are O(1)” as blanket labels. - Read one known slot: touch one element → effectively constant work. - Scan until a match / process everything: may touch up to `n` elements → O(n). - Scan an array for every item in another array: potentially `n × m` touches → O(nm). - Build a map once, then retrieve one keyed item repeatedly: one construction pass plus cheap per-key retrieval. That rule matches real programming better than calling all array access O(n), and it naturally accounts for cache and memory effects: touching more data generally means more memory traffic. You do not need to treat indexing as a strange exception. An array is explicitly built to support addressing one slot directly. The “whole array” operations are the ones that touch the whole array.
