10:53:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That analogy fits a **physical, sequential path** like a parking garage or bookshelf. It does not fit ordinary RAM addressing. A better analogy is a street address on a map: if you know “123 Main Street,” you do not need to visit addresses 1 through 122 to determine which address it is. You route to that location directly. There is still travel time, traffic, and physical distance, but not a required “check every earlier address” operation. For an array in memory, the CPU computes: ```text address = base + index × element_size ``` and requests that address. It does not step through the preceding slots. That does not mean large arrays have no performance costs: - a far-away element may not be in cache, - a page translation/cache lookup may miss, - the data may be on another NUMA node, - memory pressure may cause paging, - a loop over the whole array touches lots of cache lines. Those can make accesses slower in wall-clock time. But the usual `O(1)` claim is specifically that an indexed access does not require work proportional to the index or array length. If your storage is sequential—say a file stream, linked list, tape, or remote paginated API—then “go to item `i`” may actually be O(n). But that is not how a normal in-memory array is represented.
